---
title: "Example: access to schools and health care in Recife and Caruaru"
description: >
  From open data to accessibility for two cities of Pernambuco: an
  OpenStreetMap extract cut with osmium, the population of the Censo 2022 on
  a hexagonal grid, public schools from INEP and health units from CNES, and
  the walking, cycling and driving times to the nearest of each.
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This article is precomputed: its source is recife-caruaru.Rmd.orig, knitted
# into recife-caruaru.Rmd by `bash site/figures/make.sh --pernambuco` (the
# data takes 640 MB of downloads and osmium, too much for every site build).
# Only the chunks written out below by cat() run when pkgdown renders it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
# run in a directory where pe-data is the cache of site/figures/pernambuco-data.R
work <- file.path(tempdir(), "recife-caruaru")
dir.create(work, showWarnings = FALSE)
if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_PE_CACHE")) && !file.exists(file.path(work, "pe-data"))) {
  file.symlink(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_PE_CACHE"), file.path(work, "pe-data"))
}
knitr::opts_knit$set(root.dir = work)
# printed paths as a reader sees them, relative to the working directory
local({
  hook <- knitr::knit_hooks$get("output")
  real <- normalizePath(file.path(work, "pe-data"), mustWork = FALSE)
  knitr::knit_hooks$set(output = function(x, options) hook(gsub(real, "pe-data", x, fixed = TRUE), options))
})
live <- function(code, opts = "echo = FALSE") {
  cat("```{r, ", opts, "}\n", code, "\n```\n", sep = "")
}
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('source("setup.R")', "include = FALSE")
```

The other examples on this site use the small samples that come with r5r.
This one starts from nothing, as a study of a real city would: it downloads
the streets of two cities of Pernambuco, Brazil, from OpenStreetMap, places
their residents on a grid with the census, takes the public schools and the
primary health care units from the official registers, and measures how long
it takes to reach the nearest of each, walking, cycling and driving.

- **Recife**, the state capital (1.49 million people in the Censo 2022), on
  the coast, flat, crossed by rivers. Its street network is cut with 10 km
  around the municipality, which takes in the core of the metropolitan area
  (Olinda, Jaboatão dos Guararapes, Camaragibe and parts of Paulista and São
  Lourenço da Mata), so trips near the border can end in a neighbouring
  municipality.
- **Caruaru**, 130 km inland in the Agreste (378 thousand people), a compact
  city in a large, mostly rural municipality, with 5 km around it.

Everything below is one script, and every step works for any Brazilian
municipality: change the IBGE code and the buffer. The output shown is what
it prints; it was computed on 2026-11-11.

**Streets only.** No public, openly licensed GTFS feed was found for the
buses of the Grande Recife Consórcio de Transporte or the metro (CBTU): it is
not on the open-data portals of the city, the state or CBTU, nor in the
Mobility Database. Caruaru has none either. So this example routes on the
streets, and leaves public transport out rather than approximate it. With a
GTFS feed, the same code takes `mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT")` and a departure
time.

## The data

| Data | Source | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| Streets | [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright), extract of the Nordeste region by [Geofabrik](https://download.geofabrik.de/south-america/brazil/nordeste.html), dated 2026-11-01 (445 MB) | ODbL 1.0 |
| Municipal boundaries | [IBGE, malhas municipais](https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/docs/malhas?versao=3) (API, version 3) | public data, IBGE |
| Population | [IBGE, Censo Demográfico 2022, census tracts with their population](https://ftp.ibge.gov.br/Censos/Censo_Demografico_2022/Agregados_por_Setores_Censitarios/malha_com_atributos/setores/gpkg/UF/PE/), variable `v0001` (44 MB for Pernambuco) | public data, IBGE |
| Schools | INEP, Catálogo de Escolas 2023, with the coordinates of each school, as published by Ipea's [geobr](https://ipeagit.github.io/geobr/) (`schools_2023`, 101 MB for Brazil) | public data, INEP; geobr is MIT |
| Health units | [CNES](https://cnes.datasus.gov.br/), Ministério da Saúde, through [OpenDataSUS](https://opendatasus.saude.gov.br/) (`cnes_estabelecimentos_csv.zip`, 57 MB for Brazil, updated daily; downloaded on 2026-11-11) | open data, Ministério da Saúde |

The files are downloaded once into a directory of their own (set
`PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA` to keep it somewhere else). Cutting the extract needs
[osmium](https://osmcode.org/osmium-tool/) (`brew install osmium-tool`,
`apt install osmium-tool`, or conda-forge).

```{r download, message = FALSE}
library(peabiru)
library(sf)

data_dir <- Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA", "pe-data")
raw <- file.path(data_dir, "raw")
dir.create(raw, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
options(timeout = 3600)   # the extract is 445 MB

fetch <- function(url, name = basename(url)) {
  dest <- file.path(raw, name)
  if (!file.exists(dest)) download.file(url, dest, mode = "wb", quiet = TRUE)
  dest
}
nordeste <- fetch("https://download.geofabrik.de/south-america/brazil/nordeste-261101.osm.pbf")
tracts_file <- fetch(paste0("https://ftp.ibge.gov.br/Censos/Censo_Demografico_2022/",
                            "Agregados_por_Setores_Censitarios/malha_com_atributos/",
                            "setores/gpkg/UF/PE/PE_setores_CD2022.gpkg"))
schools_file <- fetch("https://www.ipea.gov.br/geobr/data_gpkg/schools/2023/schools_2023.gpkg")
cnes_file <- fetch(paste0("https://s3.sa-east-1.amazonaws.com/ckan.saude.gov.br/CNES/",
                          "cnes_estabelecimentos_csv.zip"))

cities <- data.frame(key = c("recife", "caruaru"),
                     code = c("2611606", "2604106"),   # IBGE codes
                     buffer = c(10000, 5000))          # metres
utm <- 31985   # SIRGAS 2000 / UTM zone 25S, in metres, for both cities
```

## Streets: the municipality and a buffer

The boundary of each municipality comes from IBGE's API. Buffered in metres,
it is the polygon `osmium extract` cuts the regional extract with; the cut
goes in a directory of its own, which `build_network()` reads.

```{r streets}
boundary_of <- function(code) {
  url <- paste0("https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/v3/malhas/municipios/", code,
                "?formato=application/vnd.geo+json&qualidade=maxima")
  b <- st_read(fetch(url, paste0(code, ".geojson")), quiet = TRUE)
  st_transform(st_make_valid(b), utm)
}

cut_streets <- function(key, boundary, buffer) {
  dir <- file.path(data_dir, key, "network")
  dir.create(dir, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
  polygon <- file.path(data_dir, key, "cut.geojson")
  st_write(st_transform(st_buffer(boundary, buffer), 4326), polygon,
           delete_dsn = TRUE, quiet = TRUE)
  pbf <- file.path(dir, paste0(key, ".osm.pbf"))
  status <- system2("osmium", c("extract", "--overwrite", "-p", polygon, nordeste, "-o", pbf))
  stopifnot(status == 0)
  dir
}

boundaries <- lapply(setNames(cities$code, cities$key), boundary_of)
networks <- list()
for (i in seq_len(nrow(cities))) {
  k <- cities$key[i]
  dir <- cut_streets(k, boundaries[[k]], cities$buffer[i])
  networks[[k]] <- build_network(dir)
  cat(sprintf("%-8s %5.1f MB\n", k, file.size(file.path(dir, paste0(k, ".osm.pbf"))) / 1e6))
}
networks$recife
networks$caruaru
```

## Where people live: a hexagonal grid with the census

The census counts people by tract, and tracts vary from a city block to
hundreds of square kilometres. A regular grid makes the cities comparable:
hexagons 500 m apart, numbered row by row from the south-west, over the
whole municipality. Each cell gets the population of the tracts it covers,
in proportion to the area it covers of each.

```{r grid}
hex_grid <- function(area, spacing = 500) {
  b <- st_bbox(area)
  h <- spacing * sqrt(3) / 2
  centres <- expand.grid(i = -1:(ceiling((b[["xmax"]] - b[["xmin"]]) / spacing) + 1),
                         j = -1:(ceiling((b[["ymax"]] - b[["ymin"]]) / h) + 1))
  centres$x <- b[["xmin"]] + centres$i * spacing + (centres$j %% 2) * spacing / 2
  centres$y <- b[["ymin"]] + centres$j * h
  k <- (30 + 60 * 0:6) * pi / 180   # pointy side up
  r <- spacing / sqrt(3)
  cells <- st_sfc(lapply(seq_len(nrow(centres)), function(n)
    st_polygon(list(cbind(centres$x[n] + r * cos(k), centres$y[n] + r * sin(k))))),
    crs = st_crs(area))
  cells <- cells[lengths(st_intersects(cells, area)) > 0]
  st_sf(id = seq_along(cells), geometry = cells)
}

population_grid <- function(code, boundary) {
  tracts <- st_read(tracts_file, quiet = TRUE, query = paste0(
    "SELECT v0001, geom FROM PE_setores_CD2022 WHERE CD_MUN = '", code, "'"))
  tracts <- st_transform(st_make_valid(tracts), utm)
  tracts$tract_area <- as.numeric(st_area(tracts))
  cells <- hex_grid(boundary)
  parts <- suppressWarnings(st_intersection(cells, tracts))
  parts$people <- parts$v0001 * as.numeric(st_area(parts)) / parts$tract_area
  people <- tapply(parts$people, parts$id, sum)
  cells$population <- 0
  cells$population[match(as.integer(names(people)), cells$id)] <- people
  centres <- st_coordinates(st_transform(st_centroid(st_geometry(cells)), 4326))
  cells$lon <- round(centres[, 1], 6)
  cells$lat <- round(centres[, 2], 6)
  cells$id <- as.character(cells$id)
  cells[cells$population > 0, ]
}

grids <- list()
for (i in seq_len(nrow(cities))) {
  k <- cities$key[i]
  grids[[k]] <- population_grid(cities$code[i], boundaries[[k]])
  cat(sprintf("%-8s %5d cells, %s people\n", k, nrow(grids[[k]]),
              format(round(sum(grids[[k]]$population)), big.mark = ",")))
}
```

The Censo 2022 counts 1,488,924 people in Recife and 378,048 in Caruaru; the
grid keeps all but 127 in Recife, in tracts that reach slightly outside
IBGE's municipal boundary. Caruaru's grid has four times as many cells as
Recife's for a quarter of the people: most of the municipality is rural, and
its large rural tracts spread a few people over each cell.

## Schools and health units

Schools: those of INEP's catalogue that are public (municipal, state or
federal) and open with no restriction, and have coordinates. Health units:
the CNES establishments of types 1 (*posto de saúde*) and 2 (*centro de
saúde / unidade básica*), the primary health care units of the SUS, that are
active and have coordinates. Both are taken over the whole area cut from
OpenStreetMap, so the nearest unit can be across the municipal border, as it
often is for people living near it.

```{r opportunities}
cut_area <- function(k) st_transform(st_read(file.path(data_dir, k, "cut.geojson"),
                                             quiet = TRUE), 4674)

schools_in <- function(area) {
  s <- st_read(schools_file, quiet = TRUE, wkt_filter = st_as_text(st_as_sfc(st_bbox(area))),
               query = paste("SELECT code_school, name_school, name_muni, geom",
                             "FROM schools_2023 WHERE admin_category = 'Pública'",
                             "AND service_restriction = 'ESCOLA EM FUNCIONAMENTO",
                             "E SEM RESTRIÇÃO DE ATENDIMENTO'"))
  s <- s[!st_is_empty(s), ]
  s <- s[lengths(st_intersects(s, area)) > 0, ]
  xy <- st_coordinates(s)
  data.frame(id = as.character(s$code_school), name = s$name_school,
             municipality = s$name_muni, lon = xy[, 1], lat = xy[, 2])
}

cnes <- read.csv2(unz(cnes_file, "cnes_estabelecimentos.csv"), fileEncoding = "latin1",
                  colClasses = "character", na.strings = character())
cnes <- cnes[cnes$CO_UF == "26" & cnes$TP_UNIDADE %in% c("1", "2") &
               cnes$CO_MOTIVO_DESAB == "" & cnes$NU_LATITUDE != "", ]
health_in <- function(area) {
  h <- data.frame(id = cnes$CO_CNES, name = cnes$NO_FANTASIA,
                  municipality_code = cnes$CO_IBGE,
                  lon = as.numeric(cnes$NU_LONGITUDE), lat = as.numeric(cnes$NU_LATITUDE))
  inside <- lengths(st_intersects(st_as_sf(h, coords = c("lon", "lat"), crs = 4674), area)) > 0
  h[inside, ]
}

schools <- lapply(setNames(cities$key, cities$key), function(k) schools_in(cut_area(k)))
health <- lapply(setNames(cities$key, cities$key), function(k) health_in(cut_area(k)))
sapply(cities$key, function(k) c(schools = nrow(schools[[k]]), health_units = nrow(health[[k]])))
```

A few things to know about these registers, seen while preparing them:

- Of the public schools in operation, 22 of 486 in Recife and 15 of 168 in
  Caruaru have no coordinates in the catalogue and cannot be placed; they are
  left out.
- Of the 167 active primary care units registered in Recife, six have
  coordinates outside the municipality: four a few hundred metres across
  the border, but one 24 km away, on the Ilha de Itamaracá, and one 20 km
  away, in Moreno. In Caruaru, two of 75 do, one of them 5 km out. Taking
  units by where they are rather than where they are registered keeps the
  far ones out of these figures (they fall outside the cut area), but those
  units are missing from their own neighbourhoods.
- Schools and health units sit close to the streets of the extract: the
  median distance to the nearest street is 7 to 16 m, and a single school
  in each city is more than 300 m from one (418 m in Recife, 574 m in
  Caruaru), well within the 1.6 km at which peabiru, like r5r, links a
  point to the street network.

## Travel time to the nearest school and health unit

One travel-time matrix per city, mode and kind of unit, from every cell to
every unit; the smallest time of each cell is its time to the nearest one.
Driving is free flow (no congestion), and nothing here models elevation yet:
Recife is flat, but Caruaru is hilly, so its walking and cycling times are
optimistic.

```{r nearest}
nearest <- function(k, mode, limit) {
  out <- st_drop_geometry(grids[[k]])
  for (what in c("schools", "health")) {
    units <- if (what == "schools") schools[[k]] else health[[k]]
    m <- travel_time_matrix(networks[[k]], out, units, mode = mode,
                            max_trip_duration = limit)
    t <- tapply(m$travel_time_p50, m$from_id, min)
    out[[what]] <- unname(t[as.character(out$id)])   # NA: none within the limit
  }
  out
}

share_within <- function(t, minutes) {
  sapply(c("schools", "health"), function(what)
    round(100 * sum(t$population[!is.na(t[[what]]) & t[[what]] <= minutes]) /
            sum(t$population), 1))
}

shares <- do.call(rbind, lapply(cities$key, function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE", "CAR"), function(mode) {
    t <- nearest(k, mode, limit = 60)
    data.frame(city = k, mode = mode,
               t(share_within(t, 15)), t(share_within(t, 30)))
  }))
}))
names(shares)[3:6] <- c("school_15", "health_15", "school_30", "health_30")
shares
```

The table is the share of the population (in %) within 15 and 30 minutes of
the nearest public school and primary health care unit. In Recife, 96% of
the people live within 15 minutes' walk of a public school and 76% of a
health unit; in Caruaru, 73% and 68%. On a bicycle the gap narrows; by car,
with free-flowing traffic, nearly everyone is within 15 minutes of both.

Recife's driving shares stop at 99.2% for a reason worth knowing: from 17
cells of Boa Viagem (about 12 thousand residents on this grid) there is no
car route to the rest of the network on this extract, in peabiru and in r5r
2.5.0 alike, although the streets connect on foot and by bicycle. Removing
every turn restriction from the extract does not reconnect them; the cause
is in how the OpenStreetMap data of that area is read for cars, and is not
identified yet. Checks like this one (cells or units that a mode never
reaches) are worth running on any new extract.

On a map, walking (drawn by `site/figures/pernambuco.R` in the repository,
from these results):

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("nearest-recife.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Two maps of Recife covered by hexagons coloured by the walking time from each cell to the nearest public school and to the nearest primary health care unit: almost every inhabited cell is within 15 minutes of a school; health units are farther apart, and cells in the south and the west are 15 to 30 minutes from the nearest one."')
live('fig("nearest-caruaru.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Two maps of the urban area of Caruaru covered by hexagons coloured by the walking time to the nearest public school and to the nearest primary health care unit: the centre and most neighbourhoods are within 10 minutes of both; the rural cells around the city are mostly within reach of a rural school, but not of a health unit."')
live('fig("access-recife-caruaru.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Bars of the share of the population of Recife and Caruaru within 15 and 30 minutes of the nearest public school and primary health care unit, walking, cycling and driving."')
```

A map like these, with plain ggplot2:

```{r map, message = FALSE}
library(ggplot2)
walk <- nearest("recife", "WALK", limit = 60)
cells <- merge(grids$recife[, "id"], walk, by = "id")
p <- ggplot(cells) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = cut(health, c(0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 60))), colour = NA) +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "OrRd", direction = -1, na.value = "grey85",
                    name = "Minutes") +
  labs(title = "Walking time to the nearest primary health care unit, Recife") +
  theme_void()
ggsave(file.path(tempdir(), "recife-health.png"), p, width = 6, height = 7, dpi = 110)
```

## How many units within reach

The nearest unit is one measure; how many units are within reach is
another, which `accessibility()` computes directly: here, the public schools
within 15 minutes' walk of each cell, and the average over the population.

```{r accessibility}
sapply(setNames(cities$key, cities$key), function(k) {
  a <- accessibility(networks[[k]], st_drop_geometry(grids[[k]]),
                     transform(schools[[k]], n = 1), opportunities_colnames = "n",
                     mode = "WALK", cutoffs = 15)
  people <- grids[[k]]$population[match(a$id, grids[[k]]$id)]
  round(weighted.mean(a$accessibility, people), 1)
})
```

On average, a resident of Recife has between four and five public schools
within a quarter of an hour on foot; one of Caruaru, between two and three.

## Isochrones from the centre

From Marco Zero, in Recife's old centre, and from the Feira de Caruaru, the
area reached in 10, 20 and 30 minutes by each mode, in square kilometres:

```{r isochrones}
centres <- list(
  recife = data.frame(id = "Marco Zero", lon = -34.8709722, lat = -8.063374),
  caruaru = data.frame(id = "Feira de Caruaru", lon = -35.9713866, lat = -8.2897512)
)
iso <- do.call(rbind, lapply(cities$key, function(k) {
  do.call(rbind, lapply(c("WALK", "BICYCLE", "CAR"), function(mode) {
    i <- isochrone(networks[[k]], centres[[k]], mode = mode,
                   cutoffs = c(10, 20, 30), zoom = 11)
    data.frame(city = k, mode = mode, minutes = i$isochrone,
               km2 = round(as.numeric(st_area(st_transform(i, utm))) / 1e6, 1))
  }))
}))
xtabs(km2 ~ mode + minutes + city, data = iso)
```

One number here depends on more than routing: the 20-minute driving polygon
of Caruaru has dozens of holes (pockets of the grid not reached in time) that
touch each other. `isochrone()` returns its polygons made valid as r5r does,
with `sf::st_make_valid()` on longitude and latitude (s2), which merges many
of those holes into the polygon: 285.9 km². Repaired with GEOS in metres
instead, as the [Python example](../../python/example-recife-caruaru/) does,
the same polygon measures 248.9 km². The other polygons agree to 0.1 km².

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("isochrones-recife.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three maps around Marco Zero, Recife, one per mode, with the areas reached within 10, 20 and 30 minutes: walking reaches the old centre and the islands next to it; cycling reaches the centre and the neighbourhoods across the rivers; driving reaches most of the map."')
live('fig("isochrones-caruaru.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three maps of the urban area of Caruaru, one per mode, with the areas reached from the Feira de Caruaru within 10, 20 and 30 minutes: walking reaches the centre, cycling most of the city, driving all of it."')
```

## Variations

- **Another municipality.** Change `cities`: the IBGE code (from
  `https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/v1/localidades/municipios`), the
  buffer, the state of the census tracts (`UF/PE/PE_setores_CD2022.gpkg`)
  and of the CNES rows (`CO_UF`), and the Geofabrik region.
- **Large matrices on disk.** With `output_dir`, `travel_time_matrix()` and
  `accessibility()` write one CSV file per origin, as r5r does, instead of
  returning a data frame: the 4,489 × 216 driving matrix of Caruaru never has
  to fit in memory.
- **Gradual access.** `decay_function = "logistic"` (or `"exponential"`,
  `"linear"`) in `accessibility()` weighs each school by its distance in time
  instead of counting those under a hard limit.
- **Public transport.** With a GTFS feed in the network directory, the same
  calls take `mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT")`, a `departure_datetime` and a
  `time_window`.
- **A browser map.** The [isochrone demo](../../demo/) has the streets of
  Recife and of the urban area of Caruaru and draws isochrones wherever you
  click.

Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors, under the ODbL; boundaries and
population © IBGE; schools: INEP (Catálogo de Escolas 2023, via geobr);
health units: CNES, Ministério da Saúde.
